<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Didda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1003</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Didda was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> from 980 to 1003, after governing as regent for her son and grandsons from 958. A princess of Lohara and wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshemagupta">Kshemagupta</a> of the Parvagupta dynasty, she outlasted rivals through intrigue and had several young kings removed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Kalhana's Rajatarangini</a> portrays her as ruthless but effective. She chose her nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangramaraja">Sangramaraja</a> as heir, founding the Lohara dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/didda.jpg" alt="Didda" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Old India Coins · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Didda_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Didda was a daughter of Simharāja, King of Lohara, and on her mother's side a granddaughter of Bhimadeva Shahi, one of the Hindu Shahi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. Lohara lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pir-panjal-range">Pir Panjal</a> range, on a trade route between western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. At the age of 26 she married Ksemagupta, King of Kashmir, uniting the two kingdoms. She had great influence on affairs even before her regency, and coins have been found that appear to bear both her name and her husband's. Most of what is known of her comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Rajatarangini</a>, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a> in the twelfth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Ksemagupta died in 958 of a fever caught after a hunt, their young son Abhimanyu II succeeded and Didda ruled as regent. She drove troublesome ministers and nobles from office, came close to losing control when they rebelled, and, with the support of others, some of them bribed, executed the captured rebels and their families. Abhimanyu died in 972 and was followed by his small son Nandigupta, which unsettled the Dāmaras, the feudatory landlords.</p><p>In 973 she disposed of Nandigupta, in the phrase of the scholar Stein, and of his younger brother Tribhuvanagupta in 975, leaving the youngest grandson Bhimagupta on the throne. After the death of the counsellor Phalunga she took as her lover a herdsman named Tunga, and in 980 had Bhimagupta tortured to death and ruled alone, with Tunga as prime minister. She and Tunga dealt with the discontent of the Dāmaras by force and diplomacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Didda died on 8 August 1003, at the age of 79. She is one of the very few female monarchs in Indian history and is sometimes called the Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Stein judged that her statesmanlike instinct and political ability, in spite of the defects of her character, showed in her remaining the last in peaceful possession of the throne and bequeathing it to her family in undisputed succession.</p><p>As heir she adopted a nephew, Samgrāmarāja, eldest son of Udayaraja, though Vigraharāja, who had murdered her parents, pressed claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and Lohara in her lifetime. She fought no wars with neighbours; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>'s expedition to Kashmir came in 1015 and failed before the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lohkot">Loharkot</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Didda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1003 Queen <b>Didda</b> of the Parvagupta dynasty was succeeded by her nephew Sangramaraja, a prince of Lohara, a hill state in the Pir Panjal range.</blockquote><a href="/state/lohara-dynasty">Lohara dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Parvagupta's son Kshemagupta married <b>Didda</b>, a princess of Lohara.</blockquote><a href="/state/parvagupta-dynasty">Parvagupta dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
